### A. Findings 1–8

I agree with 1–5, 7, and 8. Finding 6 is directionally right but overstates SQLite’s immediate-lock behavior.

1. Correct. There is no database-enforced reviewer-family floor. Guard 1 requires a version-matched pass from a subscription or metered reviewer, regardless of family ([schema.sql:779](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:779)). Guard 5 counts distinct passing reviewer seats, not families ([schema.sql:848](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:848)). Family separation is enforced only for testers and verifiers ([schema.sql:834](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:834), [schema.sql:956](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:956)). An existing test explicitly documents that same-family reviewing is permitted and claims Python will handle diversity ([test_jobs.py:222](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/tests/test_jobs.py:222)). The spec’s statement that guards 1–3 already enforce this is false; Guard 3 is about tester evidence, not reviewer diversity ([schema.sql:816](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:816)).

2. Correct. `grinder_paid` is referenced by the gauntlet ([seats.toml:342](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/config/seats.toml:342)), but the actual paid Grok seat is `grok` ([seats.toml:206](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/config/seats.toml:206)). An unknown seat will either be treated as uncapped by `over_budget()` or rejected later by `record_verdict()` ([jobs.py:356](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:356), [jobs.py:302](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:302)). That is an especially bad split: the preliminary check says “fine,” then execution fails.

3. Correct. The only in-process reviewer implementation is `claude-cli` ([executor.py:145](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:145)). The panel names Codex, nonexistent, and Ollama seats; `_run_one_review()` silently returns when no provider implementation exists ([executor.py:228](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:228)). The only usable Claude escalation seat is not in the panel ([seats.toml:253](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/config/seats.toml:253)). Therefore the proposed panel produces zero verdicts today.

4. Correct. Normal dispatch freezes `required_reviews` at the configured reviewer count—three ([dispatch.py:194](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:194)). The database demands three distinct version-matched passing reviewer seats ([schema.sql:848](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:848)) and prevents later shrinking ([schema.sql:676](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:676)). A two-family quorum cannot complete unless it also happens to contain all three configured passing seats. The spec contains two incompatible completion contracts.

5. Correct. A current-version fail is not outvotable; it blocks both `done` and `shipped` ([schema.sql:874](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:874)). Verdicts cannot change except `needs_human → pass|fail` ([schema.sql:685](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:685)). Adding a tiebreaker pass does nothing to the original fail. A contested version must be revised into a new version, not “won” by escalation. Escalation makes sense for uncertainty represented as `needs_human`, provided the design resolves that exact row through the existing resolution path ([jobs.py:326](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:326)).

6. Partly correct. Each thread needs its own connection, and write failures must not be treated as reviewer absence. But “immediately” is inaccurate: Python’s SQLite connection has a default five-second lock timeout even though this wrapper does not state one explicitly ([jobs.py:47](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:47)). WAL is enabled when the schema is initialized ([schema.sql:13](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:13)). Contention can still exceed the implicit timeout, so retries or a serialized result writer are needed. Any verdict-write failure must be returned as a panel error and prevent completion; otherwise a failed write—especially a lost fail—can disappear from the decision.

7. Correct. Production code never calls `record_usage()`; the references are tests. The hard cap applies when a usage row is inserted ([jobs.py:368](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:368), [schema.sql:979](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:979)). Merely calling `over_budget()` reserves nothing. Worse, “reserve budget” is underspecified: the system needs a conservative cost amount before the provider call and a reconciliation rule afterward. The Claude seat is uncapped in the stored budget fields, while its `max_calls_per_day` setting is neither represented by `Seat` nor synchronized into SQLite ([seats.toml:253](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/config/seats.toml:253), [dispatch.py:142](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:142)). This machinery would be safety-significant and essentially untested in the stated scope.

8. Correct. `run_gauntlet()` has no caller. The live local route explicitly requests one reviewer and fixes `required_reviews=1` ([server.py:372](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/server.py:372)). `run_job()` invokes `_run_one_review()` directly ([executor.py:210](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:210)), and a single pass attempts `done` ([executor.py:251](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:251)). The integration point must replace that step after the build is versioned and parked for review. Leaving both paths alive leaves a bypass.

### B. Minimal correct completion contract

Add an immutable `required_review_families` value to each job, snapshotted at dispatch alongside `required_reviews`. Do not have a database trigger consult current configuration: changing configuration later must not rewrite the contract for an existing job.

Completion should require all of the following for the current `head_version`:

- At least `required_reviews` distinct passing seats with `role='reviewer'`.
- At least `required_review_families` distinct passing `model_family` values with `role='reviewer'`.
- No current-version fail.
- No unresolved current-version escalation.
- For local output, at least one qualifying higher-tier pass.

Yes, the family floor belongs in a database guard. Counting “families that produced any verdict” is insufficient; completion must count passing families. Failing families already block separately.

`required_reviews` is not mathematically redundant with the family floor. Three seats could all be one family; two families could be represented by only two of a required three-seat panel. It should stay if “full set, always” remains policy, as the database comment says ([schema.sql:274](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:274)). If the actual policy is “any two families may certify despite panel dropouts,” then remove the full-panel requirement deliberately. Do not leave `required_reviews=3` and pretend the Python quorum supersedes it.

There is another subtle gap: separate conditions do not necessarily produce a single “higher-tier cross-family” reviewer. A higher-tier same-family pass plus a local different-family pass satisfies both independent conditions. If the intended contract is literally one pass that is both higher-tier and different from the builder, encode that conjunction explicitly.

### C. Risk ranking and verdict

STOP as written. It needs a corrected design before implementation.

Most dangerous first:

1. The panel has no integration point, while the old single-review path remains capable of attempting completion. More broadly, the full-panel guard runs only for transitions to `done`, not direct transitions to `shipped` ([schema.sql:850](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:850)). `ship()` does not require the prior state to be `done` ([dispatch.py:250](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:250)). That is an existing database-level bypass of the reviewer panel.

2. The asserted safety boundary does not exist. Reviewer-family coverage is only a Python convention, and the two proposed completion rules contradict one another.

3. The configured panel cannot run: one phantom seat and zero supported panel providers.

4. “Tiebreaker overturns a fail” contradicts the deliberate smoke-detector semantics of the database. Implementing it would either park every contested version or tempt someone to weaken the fail guard.

5. The database does not enforce membership in the assigned panel. Guard 5 accepts any distinct reviewer seats; three unrelated seats can fill a nominal three-seat panel ([schema.sql:856](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:856)). If the contract is “these configured reviewers,” the assigned roster must also be frozen per job.

6. Concurrent verdict recording and error handling. Lock timeouts are not immediate, but swallowed write failures can falsify the panel record.

7. Budget reservation is undefined and unexercised in scope. Call-count limits for flat-rate seats are configuration comments, not enforced state.

8. “Frozen code snapshot” is not yet a real repository snapshot. The worker hashes the generated response and writes it as a text file ([executor.py:200](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:200)); reviewers receive the request and that text, not a frozen commit or complete diff ([executor.py:228](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:228)). The database comment calls `head_version` a git commit, but the implementation currently stores a hash of model prose.

